and then I was like, um...

00:17:41.700 --> 00:17:44.566
Hmm, that makes sense. If it wears out,
it wears out—they'll replace it anyway.

00:17:44.566 --> 00:17:46.266
There's money to be made—that's
another reason.

00:17:46.433 --> 00:17:47.800
A sustainable circular economy

00:17:47.800 --> 00:17:52.633
Otherwise, it’s actually better to use a static rope,
because once the rope is secured like this, it’s actually very safe.

00:17:52.633 --> 00:17:55.366
You just need a little strength
in your hands and feet. So,

00:17:55.366 --> 00:17:56.266
what should we do next?

00:17:56.266 --> 00:18:03.800
We're about to go down this Victoria. I guess this Victoria is named after a person named Victoria who came up to climb Victoria Peak,
which is why it's called Victoria.

00:18:04.633 --> 00:18:07.533
Okay, this place is really cool—there
are hardly any trees at all.

00:18:07.533 --> 00:18:10.900
A landscape made entirely of granite—actually,
Taiwan doesn't have anything like that.

00:18:10.900 --> 00:18:13.700
So, as of the third day,
I think this trip has been

00:18:13.700 --> 00:18:15.133
I think this place is definitely
worth a visit.

00:18:15.333 --> 00:18:18.100
This hidden gem of granite behind
the Sacred Mountain—I don’t

00:18:18.100 --> 00:18:21.366
think I’ve ever seen terrain
like this in Taiwan.

00:18:21.366 --> 00:18:24.100
This place is really
worth a visit. OK.

00:18:24.100 --> 00:18:25.566
Well, let's head back down, doggie!

00:18:27.666 --> 00:18:29.100
The sun is blazing on the way down.

00:18:34.533 --> 00:18:38.000
It has great traction. NNormal Cadi—you
won't slip when you wear it like this.

00:18:47.100 --> 00:18:48.433
Why is he still so high
up there? Hahaha,

00:18:48.566 --> 00:18:50.766
Pipa the Beast James
is at the very top.

00:18:50.933 --> 00:18:54.366
The Pat
-Pat Beast hasn't moved again. Should I pat him some more??

00:18:54.466 --> 00:18:56.533
The campsite is in
a really cute spot.

00:19:03.900 --> 00:19:05.833
We can set up another belay here.

00:19:26.133 --> 00:19:27.700
You can grab these seams with your
hands. Wait a minute—on that wet,

00:19:37.966 --> 00:19:44.500
slippery section right here,
tie the same knot.

00:19:46.100 --> 00:19:50.100
If possible, you should wear
gloves—it's pretty wet.

00:20:03.500 --> 00:20:06.300
It's basically kind
of like Wuliao Jian,

00:20:06.300 --> 00:20:08.733
Below the towering cliffs—which are
actually wet—and since we’re

00:20:08.733 --> 00:20:11.766
belayed, the rope comes down like this,
so there’s no problem.

00:20:11.766 --> 00:20:13.833
We're almost at the campsite—just
this last stretch, guys!!

00:20:29.900 --> 00:20:30.966
Go help right away! Since the
guide didn't secure the rope,

00:20:34.500 --> 00:20:39.866
he tied it around his back like the last person in a tug
-of-war.

00:20:39.866 --> 00:20:43.933
And if it really does fall,
you can still use both hands to block it—I guess so.

00:20:59.333 --> 00:21:02.933
He's so happy, he's so happy. Yeah,
yesterday she was scared to death (Piggy).

00:21:03.500 --> 00:21:09.166
Back at our camp, the rest of our teammates were still climbing,
so we decided to take a short break.

00:21:14.800 --> 00:21:15.700
Just to add: Zone 4,

00:21:15.700 --> 00:21:19.000
taurine, electrolytes,
RC, green tea caffeine

00:21:19.066 --> 00:21:26.000
So now we’ve arrived at the fourth peak, Thumb Peak—that bare, rugged one over there, with its ever
-changing shape.

00:21:27.133 --> 00:21:30.033
We hiked back up from the campsite,

00:21:30.033 --> 00:21:33.200
setting out at 11:40. This is the easiest of the
Four Peaks—probably second only to Luo's Peak.

00:21:33.333 --> 00:21:37.133
Since it was just past noon, we decided to finish
the hike first and then go back for lunch.

00:21:51.600 --> 00:21:54.100
(The guide's shoes
are pretty awesome,

00:22:04.100 --> 00:22:06.300
too XD) Only six people can stand on the summit of
Thumb Peak at a time (we went up in two groups).

00:22:06.300 --> 00:22:08.700
So let's just do a quick
check to make sure.

00:22:12.233 --> 00:22:12.866
Starting lineup. Starting lineup,

00:22:13.100 --> 00:22:15.133
starting squad

00:22:15.133 --> 00:22:17.700
I had a hard time stepping on it
just now—my feet are too short.

00:22:20.300 --> 00:22:25.066
It’s actually pretty dry, so there’s enough friction,
and the shoes are actually fine.

00:22:43.166 --> 00:22:46.966
(Actually, the climb is pretty steep.

00:22:49.633 --> 00:22:53.200
) In Taiwanese, this is called a "single knot"—it
should be a single knot (so everyone can grab hold).

00:22:58.566 --> 00:23:00.266
It looks a lot like the Xiaoba.

00:23:05.700 --> 00:23:06.933
It's pretty small.

00:23:10.666 --> 00:23:15.833
3,975—another 0.

00:23:19.900 --> 00:23:20.866
9. Thumb Peak

00:23:27.200 --> 00:23:27.866
Done! Four peaks of the

00:23:28.266 --> 00:23:29.533
Sacred Mountains!

00:23:29.533 --> 00:23:31.500
The camp is down below at AVIDER.

00:23:31.500 --> 00:23:33.700
A dream come true! Thank you!

00:23:33.700 --> 00:23:35.333
Jiacheng, the best tour
leader ever! This is

00:23:35.633 --> 00:23:37.933
the second time I've
completed all four.

00:23:40.700 --> 00:23:43.733
Neither of the first two teams was able to finish—probably
because of the bad weather.

00:23:43.733 --> 00:23:47.600
But the weather has been really nice lately; we
haven't had any heavy rain these past few days.

00:23:47.600 --> 00:23:50.766
This is very similar to the summit of Xiaoba Mountain,
which is larger than this one.

00:23:50.766 --> 00:23:54.866
This is a bit narrow—very narrow,
very narrow—

00:23:54.866 --> 00:23:57.100
Yeah, this looks a lot like... I don't know,
the North Ridge, I guess.

00:24:01.600 --> 00:24:03.900
(It went smoothly—I’m
actually a little moved~~)

00:24:33.600 --> 00:24:34.700
OK, here it is.

00:24:36.633 --> 00:24:38.833
All right, they're in
this first paragraph.

00:24:44.666 --> 00:24:49.433
I watched the little horse from
afar—it was really far away.

00:24:50.800 --> 00:24:54.000
Today I hiked 3.
2K and climbed 450M before calling it a day.

00:25:00.100 --> 00:25:01.866
Thanks for your hard work.

00:25:07.300 --> 00:25:09.466
Be careful not to hit your head.
Have a good meal and enjoy a cup of tea.

00:25:09.466 --> 00:25:11.933
This is our food—salmon, salmon.

00:25:12.966 --> 00:25:15.066
Sealed Room
-Temperature Meal

00:25:15.300 --> 00:25:16.666
Kits (A whole box of eggs~~)

00:25:16.866 --> 00:25:19.266
My blood pressure is
still a bit high,

00:25:19.666 --> 00:25:22.700
haha—it almost hit
100 in an instant.

00:25:23.000 --> 00:25:24.900
(It started raining as soon as I
got back to the mountain cabin.

00:25:25.066 --> 00:25:26.366
) Little Ma is back,
soaked to the bone after his makeup exam!

00:25:26.600 --> 00:25:27.833
Wow~~~~~ He's

00:25:28.033 --> 00:25:29.300
so handsome.

00:25:30.566 --> 00:25:32.333
No one noticed my bag was outside,

00:25:32.333 --> 00:25:33.700
so now everything inside
is soaked (XDDD)

00:25:34.066 --> 00:25:35.266
(Who knows, hahaha, hehe—didn't

00:25:35.466 --> 00:25:36.666
you bring your bag?)

00:25:36.866 --> 00:25:37.233
No, I didn't.

00:25:37.366 --> 00:25:38.366
How could you not have
brought your bag?

00:25:38.433 --> 00:25:40.000
My bag has been sitting outside the whole
time. So I had some dry clothes,

00:25:40.266 --> 00:25:42.633
but now I don't know if they're
still there (what a bummer XDD)

00:25:55.800 --> 00:25:57.800
(The little book at the mountain
hut has a map of

00:26:00.433 --> 00:26:02.266
the Four Peaks!) Have a
glass of Cherry King!

00:26:03.533 --> 00:26:07.433
For this trip, we were guided by AVIDER,
a Malaysian travel agency.

00:26:07.433 --> 00:26:12.300
The Divine Mountain Range—AVIDER is mainly geared
toward some of the more challenging routes,

00:26:12.300 --> 00:26:17.100
For example, the peaks of the Sacred Mountains, as well as Mount Siguniang and the more familiar Mount Fuji,
are all included,

00:26:17.100 --> 00:26:21.300
Mount Rinjani in Indonesia, which they had visited before, is one of their top
-recommended itineraries.

00:26:21.300 --> 00:26:25.133
Well, I think the arrangements this time were really great. As for our tour leader,
Jiacheng—

00:26:25.133 --> 00:26:28.500
He is a very,
very experienced mountain guide,

00:26:28.500 --> 00:26:33.300
Then he told me that the longest mountain hike he’d ever
done took 65 days—that was in Peninsular Malaysia.

00:26:33.300 --> 00:26:37.766
Walking all the way from Phuket to the border with Malaysia
and then on to Singapore—that's so awesome!

00:26:37.766 --> 00:26:41.533
To say this is an intermediate
-level mountain trail—it’s a bit like Taiwan’s Coastal Mountain Range or something.

00:26:41.533 --> 00:26:45.033
He told them they'd have to blaze a trail as they went,
so he has a great deal of mountaineering experience.

00:26:45.033 --> 00:26:49.000
Since the Mount Kinabalu Range is located in Malaysia,
whether you're climbing Mount Kinabalu or the Mount Kinabalu Range...

00:26:49.000 --> 00:26:51.800
They all have very rigorous systems and plans,
including yours

00:26:51.800 --> 00:26:52.766
Cutoff Times—This is the first time I’ve
heard of cutoff times for mountain

00:26:52.766 --> 00:26:57.100
hikes. Hey, even Mount Fuji has them—they’re
pretty strict about it.

00:26:57.100 --> 00:27:01.233
Well, I think the best thing is that Jiacheng had us hold a very,
very detailed briefing session beforehand.

00:27:01.233 --> 00:27:02.366
So, whether it's the itinerary
for that day or the next,

00:27:02.400 --> 00:27:06.266
we're usually able to get right
into the swing of things.

00:27:06.266 --> 00:27:09.066
Because the rule here is that every
three people must have one guide,

00:27:09.066 --> 00:27:10.633
This is a local regulation,

00:27:10.633 --> 00:27:13.433
so since there are seven of us,
we’ll be assigned three local guides.

00:27:13.433 --> 00:27:17.466
With Jia Cheng as our tour leader,
this itinerary is quite comprehensive.

00:27:17.466 --> 00:27:20.833
So I’d say this AVIDER
trip was pretty chill.

00:27:20.833 --> 00:27:23.266
Let me tell you about this itinerary—it covers
the four peaks of the Sacred Mountain.

00:27:23.266 --> 00:27:30.166
I mean, given the level of mountaineering ability in Taiwan,
do you think you could organize your own group to climb the Nanhu Peaks,

00:27:30.166 --> 00:27:34.366
Well, as for these four peaks of the Sacred Mountain,
I think they’ll definitely be fine.

00:27:34.366 --> 00:27:40.266
Make sure to secure your upper body with the single rope,
and get a solid grasp of some basic concepts for climbing up and down,

00:27:40.266 --> 00:27:41.333
I don't think it'll be
much of a problem,

00:27:41.333 --> 00:27:43.033
because this route is actually
quite accessible,

00:27:43.033 --> 00:27:48.666
Personally, I think it’s on the same level as the Nanhu Peaks—a four
-day hike covering seven peaks in the Nanhu range, or something like that.

00:27:48.666 --> 00:27:53.066
This is just my personal experience—basically,
if someone takes care of my three meals a day, I don't get too tired,

00:27:53.066 --> 00:27:56.133
Since what I'm carrying isn't that heavy,
the main thing is acclimating to the altitude,

00:27:56.133 --> 00:28:01.166
Because the altitude here is really high, we’re currently at 3,
850 meters, where it’s very, very easy to get altitude sickness,

00:28:01.166 --> 00:28:07.666
For example, this morning one of our team members suffered from altitude sickness,
so it’s essential to stay warm and eat well.

00:28:07.666 --> 00:28:11.466
I’d like to emphasize once again that you should
be sure to bring plenty of warm clothing.

00:28:11.466 --> 00:28:16.400
Make sure your mid
-layer is thick enough—no matter what month you plan to climb this range of sacred peaks, you’ll need a down jacket.

00:28:16.400 --> 00:28:18.400
You should definitely bring
the thickest one.

00:28:18.400 --> 00:28:21.933
If you’re really sensitive to the cold,
you can still bring your own thick sleeping bag,

00:28:21.933 --> 00:28:26.466
Then you can have AVIDER arrange for your
porter to carry this sleeping bag for you.

00:28:26.466 --> 00:28:32.300
So I just mentioned a few itineraries, including the Kumano Kodo,
as well as the ABC and EBC—basically, those are the standard itineraries in Nepal.

00:28:32.300 --> 00:28:34.900
They also carry AVIDER,
so you can check it out.

00:28:34.900 --> 00:28:37.833
I think what stood out the most
was during our trip abroad

00:28:37.833 --> 00:28:41.600
Their biggest strength is actually that
they can all communicate in Chinese.

00:28:41.600 --> 00:28:46.766
So their guide will definitely speak both Chinese and Malay,
so you’ll have absolutely no problem climbing Mount Kinabalu.

00:28:46.766 --> 00:28:52.633
If you join one of their tours overseas, they’ll definitely assign a Chinese
-speaking tour guide to help you communicate.

00:28:52.966 --> 00:28:57.433
Well, if you have a specific idea in mind—if there’s
a hidden gem you’re really eager to visit—

00:28:57.433 --> 00:29:01.066
You can also ask AVIDER to customize
this itinerary for you,

00:29:01.066 --> 00:29:04.766
They are all capable of designing customized
hiking routes like this.

00:29:04.766 --> 00:29:07.500
I have one on my head right now. Oh,
wait—like this, there are only two,

00:29:07.500 --> 00:29:11.033
As for the itinerary, I think it’s a bit like the Big and Little Ba Mountains—it’s a two
-night, three-day trek with a similar level of difficulty,

00:29:11.033 --> 00:29:17.300
If you’re capable of traveling independently in Taiwan, I personally think the Shen Shan Mountain Range is a route that’s very,
very worth visiting.

00:29:17.300 --> 00:29:22.800
Since I'm not particularly interested in Mount Kamiyama itself,
but because I first heard about this AVIDER group of Kamiyama peaks back then—

00:29:22.800 --> 00:29:26.033
So, wow, I didn't realize there was
a whole other world behind this.

00:29:26.033 --> 00:29:32.066
Because I originally thought I could wait until I was a little older to visit Kamiyama and places like Omote
-Ginza in Japan.

00:29:32.066 --> 00:29:36.433
However, when it comes to the Shen Mountain range,
I would personally recommend doing it now, since it’s already possible.

00:29:36.933 --> 00:29:41.666
How can I put it? Perhaps a level equivalent to climbing 50 of the "Hundred
Peaks" would be sufficient to take on the "Divine Mountains" range.

00:29:41.666 --> 00:29:45.500
Because the trails here are completely different from those
in Taiwan—it’s like a magical world of granite.

00:29:45.500 --> 00:29:48.633
This route just opened this year,
so I think it has a bright future.

00:29:48.633 --> 00:29:55.433
This also includes the new mountain hut that’s currently being built,
as mentioned earlier, so the Malaysian government may gradually

00:29:55.433 --> 00:29:58.633
To expand the limits of
climbing these peaks

00:29:58.633 --> 00:30:02.300
So if you're really planning to visit Qunfeng,
I'd suggest you come right away—don't hesitate.

00:30:02.300 --> 00:30:04.900
I rarely recommend something this much—because
the terrain and trails here are unlike

00:30:04.900 --> 00:30:09.700
anything you’d see in Taiwan; they’re
incredibly beautiful and spectacular.

00:30:09.800 --> 00:30:11.766
OK, so we'll have nothing to do later
XDD We're enjoying an amazing

00:30:11.766 --> 00:30:15.633
view and great weather,
and we're about to have dinner—woof!

00:30:15.633 --> 00:30:18.966
So let me emphasize once again that sun
protection is extremely important.

00:30:18.966 --> 00:30:23.333
Since this is a high
-altitude trek, it's best to use both sunscreen and physical sun protection.

00:30:23.333 --> 00:30:27.966
So I highly recommend bringing an umbrella,
because it rains very, very easily here in the afternoons.

00:30:27.966 --> 00:30:31.266
We were very lucky this time—we finished
most of our itinerary before noon,

00:30:31.266 --> 00:30:34.566
It was finished before 1 or 2 o'clock,
and then the rain didn't start falling until after 2 or 3 o'clock.

00:30:34.566 --> 00:30:35.600
Yiwei Neng, Good Sleep Capsules.

00:30:36.000 --> 00:30:38.700
Just take two every night.

00:30:38.700 --> 00:30:43.733
From boarding and deplaning to hiking—I'll be using this for the next three days,
through tomorrow.

00:30:43.733 --> 00:30:46.233
This Japanese
-patented tryptophan helps you fall asleep.

00:30:46.366 --> 00:30:50.733
In that case, you can try some other ways to help you sleep
better—but the most important thing is to stay warm.

00:30:54.833 --> 00:30:56.700
I just introduced AVIDER (a must
-have DSLR for every tour leader)—it really takes great photos.

00:30:56.700 --> 00:31:01.366
Look at him—he’s turned into a “Pat
-Pat Beast” now, crouching like that, and his thighs don’t even get sore.

00:31:01.666 --> 00:31:03.333
That's insane! Just what you'd

00:31:03.333 --> 00:31:06.933
expect from AVIDER Fengkuang!

00:31:08.166 --> 00:31:09.533
This is what we look like now,

00:31:12.766 --> 00:31:16.066
and we have apples, too—so we’ll have
a better meal for our last supper.

00:31:18.033 --> 00:31:20.000
He scrambled both cartons of eggs.

00:31:20.300 --> 00:31:20.633
What is this?

00:31:20.766 --> 00:31:23.633
This is a sauce—a thick soup,

00:31:23.633 --> 00:31:24.933
I guess. It looks like
some kind of fish cake?

00:31:25.633 --> 00:31:28.466
Chicken breast again—we have this at every meal,
and I'm starting to get a little sick of it.

00:31:29.833 --> 00:31:30.533
The Last Supper.

00:31:31.466 --> 00:31:33.833
(Nepalese curved dagger)

00:31:48.200 --> 00:31:49.733
First thing in the morning,

00:32:01.000 --> 00:32:02.400
"Flowing Hair Guy" was already combing his hair.
There were so many people at the summit.

00:32:09.000 --> 00:32:13.700
This is the first time I've brought this SmartWater
bottle—I brought it back from the U.S.

00:32:13.900 --> 00:32:16.200
(A Must
-Have Water Bottle for Lightweight Hiking) So,

00:32:19.366 --> 00:32:22.633
this time I’m using the
PAAGO ZENN 45L again.

00:32:24.166 --> 00:32:29.266
Malay Bird. That little Malay bird is

00:32:33.000 --> 00:32:35.300
so chubby. It's totally overpowered!

00:32:41.833 --> 00:32:42.933
So awesome~~ We set out from the campground
under a blazing sun—it was super

00:32:54.300 --> 00:33:00.000
hot—and headed to the mountain hut
halfway up the mountain for brunch.

00:33:00.266 --> 00:33:05.200
It wasn't this cold yesterday—I actually sweated while I was sleeping,
so I wore two layers.

00:33:05.733 --> 00:33:09.300
One STATIC OCTA, one Alpha with down,
and the thickest down jacket from Kawas—the Poway

00:33:09.300 --> 00:33:11.800
I didn't zip up my sleeping bag.

00:33:11.800 --> 00:33:14.900
It rained quite a bit and was pretty cold the night before,
but yesterday was fine.

00:33:16.500 --> 00:33:20.333
Set off, back to the 8.

00:33:30.766 --> 00:33:34.933
5K fork in the road. We returned to the 8.5K mark,
St. John's

00:33:36.533 --> 00:33:37.366
Nanfeng mentioned yesterday that,

00:33:47.300 --> 00:33:50.566
among the recommended items, the one you should
definitely bring is a “water filter.”

00:33:50.800 --> 00:33:56.100
In my view, when it comes to mountain climbing—whether it’s independent travel,
hiring a guide, joining a tour group, or wherever you go—

00:33:56.100 --> 00:34:00.800
A water filter can basically be thought of as a bag for holding water,
and besides, the filter head isn't that heavy.

00:34:00.800 --> 00:34:06.200
You can collect rainwater anytime—just like right here where we are now,
with a clear view of Tianchi Lake.

00:34:06.200 --> 00:34:08.733
You really don't have any water (so you can fill it up). This is very,
very important.

00:34:08.733 --> 00:34:11.666
Without water,
you'd really be done for in three days.

00:34:12.100 --> 00:34:16.633
Even if they (the collaborators) can cook, I think having a water filter
makes it really convenient to drink water whenever you want.

00:34:16.633 --> 00:34:19.300
Since they’ll say not to bring it,
I’ll explain it to everyone in this video

00:34:19.300 --> 00:34:23.433
Last time in Omotesando, and this time in Kamiyama—I’ve
always recommended that you bring a water filter.

00:34:23.433 --> 00:34:26.900
If you are an experienced hiker

00:34:26.900 --> 00:34:28.866
I'm sure you'll know what
I'm talking about.

00:34:29.033 --> 00:34:30.866
The second item is “slippers.” Since
this campout is two days long,

00:34:30.933 --> 00:34:35.033
I feel pretty lucky that
it didn’t rain.

00:34:35.033 --> 00:34:37.800
So there's no problem with wearing
these shoes as hiking boots.

00:34:37.800 --> 00:34:42.066
But if it rains, you might want to bring a pair of slippers as a backup,
just in case.

00:34:42.433 --> 00:34:46.133
I brought a titanium stove with me—you
really need to cook something.

00:34:46.133 --> 00:34:49.833
Since we were at the foot of the mountain, he said that almost
none of the outdoor gear stores here sell (propane).

00:34:49.833 --> 00:34:53.966
I should say we didn't go to any stores that carried a wide selection of gear,
and in the end, we didn't buy any gas.

00:34:54.100 --> 00:34:57.466
Well, if you really need it,
borrow it from Xiezuo,

00:34:57.466 --> 00:34:59.366
They have plenty of gas,
so you can go cook.

00:34:59.366 --> 00:35:01.600
I haven't used it at all
these past few days,

00:35:01.600 --> 00:35:04.300
that's right. But since that titanium
burner weighs 25 grams, so

00:35:04.600 --> 00:35:07.433
Whether to carry a spare is really up to the individual,
you know,

00:35:07.433 --> 00:35:11.966
I just thought I'd play it safe,
so I brought a portable stove with me.

00:35:11.966 --> 00:35:15.766
There are four peaks in the Divine Mountain Range.
Check out the map in our community group.

00:35:15.766 --> 00:35:20.100
Since this information is basically hard to find online,
I’ll organize it a bit more clearly and share it with everyone.

00:35:20.400 --> 00:35:22.900
The four mountains near the campsite—yes,
that's right.

00:35:23.766 --> 00:35:26.966
When I looked at it yesterday,
all the distances were less than about 1 kilometer.

00:35:26.966 --> 00:35:31.700
But since it's all uphill—all uphill—the elevation gain is a bit steep,
but the distance isn't very long.

00:35:31.966 --> 00:35:34.200
For this tour of the Four Peaks
of the Sacred Mountain range,

00:35:34.200 --> 00:35:38.366
you can contact the Malaysian travel agency
AVIDER—they’re quite experienced.

00:35:38.566 --> 00:35:42.766
Okay, as for my overall impression this time,
I personally found it very comfortable.

00:35:42.766 --> 00:35:44.400
And what does “comfort” mean,

00:35:44.633 --> 00:35:48.733
anyway? It means that when I wake up every day,
my body doesn’t feel too tired, because...

00:35:48.800 --> 00:35:52.800
Eat well, sleep well,
and the entire route shouldn't be too difficult.

00:35:52.800 --> 00:35:58.133
We followed AVIDER's itinerary, and they also held a very detailed pre
-trip briefing beforehand.

00:35:58.266 --> 00:36:02.433
If you're afraid of heights,
but let's say, for example—

00:36:02.433 --> 00:36:08.200
Have you ever climbed Xiaoba Jianshan in Taiwan? Have you ever climbed the East Peak
of Yushan in Taiwan? Have you ever climbed the North Peak of Qilai in Taiwan?

00:36:08.200 --> 00:36:13.200
These ones—oh, you have some relevant experience; you’ve even climbed Yinzui and Wuliao Jian,
among others.

00:36:13.533 --> 00:36:16.800
In that case, I don't think there
will be any major problems.

00:36:16.800 --> 00:36:21.233
In terms of physical fitness, that would be the three
-day, two-night hike through the Big and Little Ba Mountains I mentioned earlier—you could also tackle the main and northern peaks of the Qilai Mountains.

00:36:21.366 --> 00:36:26.066
I think the hardest part was probably the weather—it
was absolutely beautiful this time.

00:36:26.066 --> 00:36:27.800
So it went very smoothly.
At the very least,

00:36:27.800 --> 00:36:35.133
if you have some experience sleeping in a tent
overnight in the high mountains of Taiwan,

00:36:35.133 --> 00:36:36.666
I don't think the Shen Mountain
range is really that

00:36:36.766 --> 00:36:40.400
difficult—or too difficult—of a hike,
you know.

00:36:40.533 --> 00:36:43.900
OK, the PAAGO WORKS hiking backpack—I’ve
used this one in Japan.

00:36:43.900 --> 00:36:46.733
Just a quick correction for everyone—I
made a mistake last time.

00:36:46.733 --> 00:36:49.600
It's the position of the chest strap—last
time I moved it too high.

00:36:49.600 --> 00:36:54.233
I always thought the chest strap went here, but it's actually
designed to sit just below the bottom of your nipples.

00:36:54.233 --> 00:36:58.833
Below this point is the lower part of the breast,
below the line connecting the nipples.

00:36:59.100 --> 00:37:00.966
I felt really,

00:37:01.366 --> 00:37:04.100
really comfortable this time. Maybe it’s because I didn’t bring
my camera—it just felt like I was carrying over 1 kilogram less.

00:37:04.100 --> 00:37:05.833
I actually think there’s
quite a big difference.

00:37:05.900 --> 00:37:08.466
I think 45 liters is
just right for me,

00:37:08.466 --> 00:37:12.866
Since I’ve always used a 38
-liter one before, 38 liters is actually perfectly fine, and it’s lighter when empty.

00:37:12.866 --> 00:37:15.133
I think the Bonfus 38L Iterus would be
better off as a 45L model—it would

00:37:15.133 --> 00:37:19.933
give you more room to pack some potato chips,
snacks, and other bulky items.

00:37:19.933 --> 00:37:22.066
If you have some items that are bulky but not very heavy,
you can just stuff them in like this.

00:37:22.066 --> 00:37:23.100
White~~~ It's just so cool,

00:37:23.700 --> 00:37:25.066
so...

00:37:25.800 --> 00:37:27.033
We still win with our
looks! WIN. Well,

00:37:27.200 --> 00:37:30.733
if you want to make friends with these cool
guides—the "Flowing Hair Guys"—

00:37:30.733 --> 00:37:34.800
Definitely! I suggest you bring some
hard liquor in your titanium flask.

00:37:35.200 --> 00:37:39.833
Actually, just think of him as one of the local mountain guides
here—someone with the feel of a Taiwanese indigenous person.

00:37:39.833 --> 00:37:42.733
They're actually pretty laid
-back, too—at night, they just hang out and chat.

00:37:42.733 --> 00:37:46.166
Once I'm done with all these little tasks,
I'll have nothing to do.

00:37:46.166 --> 00:37:49.066
He said they forgot to bring alcohol this time,
and they usually drink hard liquor anyway.

00:37:49.066 --> 00:37:50.866
So I'd suggest you
bring some whiskey.

00:37:51.166 --> 00:37:53.266
Or, if it's Taiwan's 58 Kaoliang,
you don't have to drink it yourself—you

00:37:53.266 --> 00:37:59.433
can offer it to our three
handsome guides instead.

00:37:59.433 --> 00:38:04.433
He'll give you more hands
-on guidance so you can have a better time on the hike.

00:38:04.433 --> 00:38:09.033
These all
-carbon-fiber hiking poles are really lightweight.

00:38:09.033 --> 00:38:13.033
Each one weighs a little over 100 grams,
as long as you apply the force at the right angle.

00:38:13.033 --> 00:38:16.566
Next, thrust downward—whether you're going uphill or downhill,
thrust downward.

00:38:16.566 --> 00:38:19.333
Basically, there shouldn't
be any major problems.

00:38:19.333 --> 00:38:22.300
Just to clarify, I personally push my trekking
poles into the ground with a lot of force.

00:38:22.300 --> 00:38:24.600
So when I go hiking, my hands get really sore,
too—but there’s nothing I can do about it; it’s just too light.

00:38:25.000 --> 00:38:27.633
Because, actually, the heavier the hiking poles are,
the more tired your hands will get.

00:38:27.633 --> 00:38:33.966
Since this swing with each step also consumes more energy, it’s actually
a good idea to choose a relatively lightweight version of hiking poles.

00:38:33.966 --> 00:38:37.666
As for how light it should be, I think that depends
entirely on how you use it and the route you take.

00:38:37.666 --> 00:38:43.600
We're at South Peak, and fog is rolling in. We're going to head down now to the 6K mark. Okay,
let's keep going.

00:38:44.366 --> 00:38:45.866
Let's head downhill,

00:39:06.400 --> 00:39:08.166
dog! Trailrunner!

00:39:08.433 --> 00:39:09.666
Dang! Three hours!!!!!! (Beat

00:39:25.933 --> 00:39:31.166
the top
-ranked ITA!)

00:39:34.100 --> 00:39:35.366
A Cabin on the Hillside! The

00:39:36.433 --> 00:39:38.233
World's Highest Mailbox

00:39:39.400 --> 00:39:40.266
Send this to my son! Tell him to come

00:39:41.400 --> 00:39:42.700
back and climb it in 20 years.

00:39:42.866 --> 00:39:45.266
That banner is for applying
for trail running training.

00:39:46.200 --> 00:39:49.466
Breakfast: cereal and milk.

00:39:49.566 --> 00:39:50.566
Oh my gosh!

00:39:51.166 --> 00:39:51.633
Breakfast. It was really good! So
this is what it actually looked

00:39:51.866 --> 00:39:54.633
like at first (we didn't get
to eat it at our last meal).

00:39:55.233 --> 00:39:56.833
This is what I look like when
I haven't had breakfast.

00:39:56.866 --> 00:39:57.266
Thanks.

00:39:57.400 --> 00:39:59.566
So much! Oh,

00:40:00.766 --> 00:40:01.400
scrambled eggs~~ Pancakes.

00:40:11.400 --> 00:40:14.333
If you add milk to coffee that’s already five times as diluted,
it’ll get even thinner (super thin, haha).

00:40:14.533 --> 00:40:15.166
It's pretty tasty.

00:40:15.333 --> 00:40:17.700
And the person across from me
is mixing concrete (?????

00:40:17.700 --> 00:40:21.333
He's going to work in a little while; he's
going to build a toilet in a little while.

00:40:21.333 --> 00:40:23.366
It looks just like concrete!
And it's almost done,

00:40:24.633 --> 00:40:25.433
almost done.

00:40:25.700 --> 00:40:27.500
Oh, it's sticky, sticky, sticky.

00:40:27.500 --> 00:40:29.900
I’d like to go ahead and recommend
Sifeng to everyone. Why?

00:40:29.900 --> 00:40:31.466
I think you should be able
to see it. Usually,

00:40:33.100 --> 00:40:36.633
when people climb that sacred mountain,
they only see Luo Peak.

00:40:36.633 --> 00:40:39.333
Once you enter the campground, you can fully immerse
yourself in the experience of the sacred mountain.

00:40:39.533 --> 00:40:43.333
You can see the "Golden Mountain in the Sun" from Luo Shi Peak,
and you can see the "Golden Mountain in the Sun" from other mountains as well.

00:40:43.333 --> 00:40:47.600
At night, you can wait for that starry sky—I think it's really,
really nice.

00:40:47.600 --> 00:40:52.200
So many—we really saw so many stars,
but we were just really lucky; the weather was just perfect.

00:40:52.200 --> 00:40:53.100
Climbing the Peaks: What do you think

00:40:53.100 --> 00:40:55.500
is AVIDER’s biggest advantage?

00:40:55.500 --> 00:41:01.166
I'd say what we offer is, um, very convenient—all you have to do is show up with your bags,
and we'll take care of everything for you.

00:41:01.633 --> 00:41:04.800
Tents, sleeping bags, sleeping pads,
and three meals a day for all of you

00:41:04.800 --> 00:41:06.966
We've taken care of all
the paperwork for you.

00:41:06.966 --> 00:41:09.766
You can rest assured—just join the
tour without a care in the world.

00:41:09.900 --> 00:41:12.766
It was really mindless, and I have to emphasize
again that we were absolutely stuffed.

00:41:12.766 --> 00:41:14.333
Since we were really worried we
wouldn't get enough to eat,

00:41:14.333 --> 00:41:18.500
Jiacheng told us to eat as much as we wanted,
and we never actually finished a single meal—hahaha.

00:41:18.666 --> 00:41:20.900
So, if you come here,
you can look for AVIDER's itineraries like this.

00:41:23.800 --> 00:41:25.500
Quick question: What should
I eat on the way down? Just

